1	// Composio owns handle collection for LinkedIn, Instagram and X. Their
2	// managed OAuth apps return what these platforms hide from Clerk's own
3	// connections -- proven live: LINKEDIN_GET_MY_INFO answers with vanityName
4	// ("tony-buildd"), profileUrl and a profilePicture.displayImage, and
5	// INSTAGRAM_GET_USER_INFO answers with username ("t_n1706") and a
6	// profile_picture_url for a creator/business account. Clerk keeps signing
7	// people in; this file never touches identity, only the one verified handle
8	// -- and, where the same call proves one, the one photo -- a connected
9	// account gives up. It never downloads the photo itself: iOS gets the URL
10	// back and imports it, the same handoff the old Clerk flow used.
11	//
12	// REST via fetch, mirroring emailClient.ts / openaiClient.ts rather than
13	// adding a Composio SDK dependency for three endpoints. Shapes below are
14	// pinned against the live OpenAPI document at
15	// https://backend.composio.dev/api/v3/openapi.json (checked 2026-08-04), not
16	// against prose docs -- the hosted docs site did not reliably resolve to the
17	// exact request/response fields, and the spec is the one thing that cannot
18	// drift from what the API actually accepts.
